forget everything you've heard about the  endometriosis diet i'm here to tell you  the truth hey everyone i'm abby sharp  welcome to abby's kitchen  [Music]  today's video is sponsored by buildbar  and we'll be chatting the truth about  the food rules that influencers love to  dole out for endometriosis but first let  me tell you about my sponsor build bar  if you have yet to try my go-to favorite  protein bar get on it i don't know about  you guys but i hate most protein bars on  the market because they're just kind of  chalky and gritty but build bars have a  texture kind of like nougat or like a  thick marshmallow and it's coated in  actual chocolate so because your girl is  having a rough day here today's flavor  is my favorite cookie dough chunk hello  never gets old never  oh yeah  oh  you can see those little pieces joe  [Music]  15 grams of protein and they are a lower  sugar bar so if you want to build into a  hunger crushing combo i love to pair  mine with like oatmeal or toast or fruit  so good so if you want to check out  built bars for yourself hit up the link  in the description and use my promo code  abbysharp15 to get 15 off of your order  and you can pause the screen or look in  the description to check out my  disclaimer including a trigger warning  to those with current or previous  experiences with disordered eating and  if you are not already subscribed hit  that sub button and follow me on tik tok  and instagram abby's kitchen alright so  endo friends tell me all the time that  they are easily roped in by snake oil  because the wellness industry just loves  to prey on folks in chronic pain but if  you don't already know endometriosis is  a chronic estrogen dependent  inflammatory condition affecting one in  nine women of reproductive age basically  the endometrial tissue starts to grow  outside the uterus which results in  painful periods painful sex painful  bowel movements and urination as well as  heavy periods fatigue ibs symptoms  infertility and increased risk of  ovarian cancer now because of its  hormonal and inflammatory origin a lot  of the foods commonly demonized in the  wellness community for folks with  endometriosis are those that are thought  to be pro-inflammatory  those that dysregulate hormones like  estrogen or those that just exacerbate  digestive symptoms and pain but i spoke  to some of my colleagues who  specifically work in endometriosis to  get the real facts straight so let's  start with the common foods that you've  been told to avoid and what the research  actually says first up fried foods now  most diets on my radar discourage ultra  processed fried foods and in the case of  endometriosis the rationale is to reduce  the consumption of trans fats which  prospective research has associated with  endometriosis risk now while trans fats  were banned in north america in 2018  they are still found in highly processed  fried foods because the high cooking  temperature slightly increases the trans  fat content of oil as does cooling and  reheating the same oil so choosing foods  that are grilled baked poached or  roasted instead of fried more often may  help and of course we also want to be  mindful of consuming a lot of packaged  snacks with the words partially  hydrogenated oils which is essentially  code for small amounts of trans fats now  you guys know i am not one to suggest  cutting out foods but trans fats are  basically one of the only food  components we definitively know are not  great for our health even in moderate  amounts next is red meat now this is  another not so shocking recommendation  but red meats are believed to play a  role in endometriosis due to their  saturated fat natural steroid hormone  content or the cooking methods used  which increases advanced glycation end  products one fairly large study did see  an association with frequent consumption  of red meat though other studies have  kind of muddled the waters by finding no  increased risk but a more recent  prospect of study on over 80 000 women  found that women who consumed more than  two servings of red meat daily had a 56  higher risk of endometriosis and  specifically had more pelvic pain they  did not find that poultry fish seafood  or eggs to have the same effect so  focusing on protein intake with those  options plus of course some plant-based  proteins may be a supportive swap next  is another heavy hitter on the foods  with a bad pr list gluten but this time  it may actually be somewhat  evidence-based now one study looked at  over 200 women with endometriosis and  found that a gluten-free diet  significantly reduced pelvic pain pain  during sex and period pain other  research found that patients who avoided  wheat products have better pain scores  and subjective well-being scores than  those who don't now we don't know for  sure why this is but it's possible that  less gluten means fewer ultra-processed  foods or fewer fodmaps which explains  why a lot of my colleagues find that  switching to less refined whole grains  may actually be more effective than  completely cutting them out but that  brings me to the next diet  recommendation low fodmap now some  endometriosis symptoms like abdominal  pain constipation nausea bloating and  diarrhea often cause women to be  misdiagnosed with ibs before finally  getting a proper endo diagnosis still  there does seem to be some overlap in  these conditions and those with ibs  symptoms do report feeling better on a  modified or low fodmap diet note that  the low fodmap diet is not really like  one diet each person will have their own  unique fodmap triggers so always work at  the gi dietitian on a proper fodmap  elimination and trial and you can check  out my own fodmap experience in my video  right here next let's talk about alcohol  so what we know is that alcohol raises  estrogen levels which then thickens the  endometrium and this ultimately results  in more painful endo symptoms one review  of the literature found an association  between alcohol use and endometriosis  with one study finding that 50 of women  reported adverse effects when they drink  does that mean you need to maintain  absolute abstinence well while a  meta-analysis did conclude that any  amount of alcohol seemed to increase  risk other research has found no link at  all so while it doesn't seem like  alcohol causes endo i think it's worth  experimenting to see if having an  occasional celebratory drink or weekend  glass of wine exacerbates a flare for  you you can work out the unique risk  benefit analysis for yourself now like  alcohol caffeine also increases estrogen  levels in some populations which seem to  actually be race dependent but of course  this has resulted in a universal  recommendation in the endo community to  limit the morning joe got you a  cappuccino with three red bulls and  while some research has found an  association with coffee intake and  endometriosis diagnosis others don't  seem to find any strong association at  all of course because of the association  between gi or ibs symptoms and endo some  folks may find it triggers a flare so  again we want to experiment to determine  your unique tolerance to caffeine  products and to find an amount if any  that works for you okay so we got  through the not so great news but i  really want to focus our attention on  what we can add to the diet and the good  news is that there's an abundance of  food that may help to support symptoms  and reduce risk now since endometriosis  is considered an inflammatory condition  the general recommendations are all  really going to focus on foods that have  those anti-inflammatory properties most  notably fruits and veg i think most  diets are going to recommend more fruits  and vegetables for really a wide range  of reasons and in the case of  endometriosis the fiber here seems to  reduce estrogen levels while the  antioxidant levels seem to decrease  oxidative stress and other markers of  inflammation most of the research has  found associations between a diet rich  in fruits and vegetables and a reduced  endo risk but specific veggies may  potentially exacerbate symptoms likely  related to fodmap content so this is  where the recommendations can feel a  little bit contradictory and maybe  overwhelming because as we already  discussed  some folks with endo will find that a  gluten-free diet may be best but at the  same time we need to balance this  recommendation with the fiber benefits  of whole grains so again as always work  with a dietitian to determine which  sources of fodmaps and which sources of  fiber are best tolerated for you and  which help to actually help to reduce  symptoms and pain and speaking of  complicated nutrition iron is another  more sticky topic in the endo community  so old-school endometriosis thinking  related to the retrograde menstruation  theory was concerned with evidence on  iron overload in the pelvic cavity which  is thought to play a role in  endometriosis and symptoms but newer  research and clinical observation is  much more concerned with the increased  risk of iron deficiency anemia because  of the heavy periods associated with the  disorder now throw in the positive  association with red meat consumption  and endo which is really like our best  most bioavailable source of iron and  it's honestly confusing as  what we know here is that iron can have  a significant impact on endometriosis  cell gene expression so maintaining  healthy iron levels may help to reduce  symptoms so step one is to have your  iron levels checked regularly by your  doctor to make sure they are within  healthy range and if you're looking low  to focus on non-red meat sources of iron  so that could be fish seafood poultry  and eggs for your hem-based iron or  things like tofu beans nuts seeds and  greens for your non-heme iron ideally  pairing those with some vitamin c rich  fruits and vegetables to improve  absorption and speaking of fish an extra  benefit of including more seafood in our  diet is their anti-inflammatory omega-3  content so research suggests that higher  amounts of epa omega-3s which are found  in salmon tuna mackerel sardines and  algae is linked to less severe symptoms  and pelvic pain likely due to its impact  on prostaglandins so if you enjoy fish  this is a great anti-inflammatory staple  to swap in for protein and iron and if  you don't eat fish an algae-based  supplement may also help now another  controversial and confusing food out  there that is often demonized in some  endo circles is soy and soy based  products now you will absolutely find  influencers out there insisting that you  need to cut out soy since soy contains  compounds called phytoestrogens which  essentially can mimic estrogen in the  body and again since endometriosis is a  hormonal condition this is deemed a  risky choice but while one study found  women who were fed soy formula as kids  had double the risk of endometriosis as  those who didn't the vast majority of  research has actually found it to have a  beneficial impact one study found soy  was not associated with risk of  endometriosis while others have found  that it may actually help to decrease  symptoms now the super simplified  explanation for this very complicated  phenomenon is that phytoestrogens are  weaker than estrogen and when they bind  the estrogen receptors it leaves fewer  spots available for the og estrogen to  bind and this results in a lower  estrogenic effect which may help to down  regulate some of the estrogen-dependent  symptoms in fact one phytoestrogen  called purarin is currently being  investigated as a potential  endometriosis treatment having said that  again this is an individualized thing  since soy contains fodmaps and some  folks symptoms may therefore be  triggered so experiment to see if you  feel better with or without it in the  mix finally let's talk dairy i know  wellness culture usually likes on dairy  but one prospective study found that  both intakes of total and low-fat dairy  independently were associated with a  lower risk of endometriosis women who  are consuming more than three servings  of total dairy per day were 18  less likely to be diagnosed with  endometriosis than those consuming two  or less a meta-analysis confirmed this  suggesting that women consuming three  servings or more had a reduced risk  particularly with higher fat dairy and  cheese but not with butter so ideally we  want to look for vitamin d fortified  dairy since as we'll discuss in a moment  vitamin d deficiency may also put one at  greater risk and to round out this  discussion let's talk supplements again  the focus of the limited research to  date has been on antioxidant based  supplements to counter the chronic  inflammatory condition we know through  correlational research that women with  endometriosis  tend to have lower intakes of  antioxidants like vitamins a c e zinc  and copper than women without one study  testing the impact of supplementation  found that women taking 1200 iu's of  vitamin e and a thousand ius of vitamin  c had a reduction in chronic pain pain  during menstruation and pain during sex  there was also a decline in quantifiable  inflammatory markers compared to the  placebo group we also have early  research on the antioxidant rich  compound curcumin from turmeric which  found that supplementing helped to  suppress the proliferation of  endometrial cells by reducing the  hormone estradiol finally prospective  data suggests that women with the  highest levels of vitamin d have 24  lower risk of endometriosis than women  with the lowest levels so it is possible  that vitamin d may be supportive as well  so to summarize the diet recommendations  for endometriosis are really not  surprising  i mean i'm shocked we're dealing with a  chronic inflammatory condition so a diet  that's rich in anti-inflammatory  compounds like you know antioxidants and  omega-3s and low-in pro-inflammatory  compounds like trans fats seems to be  the most supportive also like all diets  this appears to be highly individualized  so what works for one person might not  have much an effect for another and vice  versa so as always definitely speak to a  dietitian about your unique case and on  that note that's all that i have for you  guys today thank you again to buildbar  for sponsoring if you like this video be  sure to give it that thumbs up leave me  a comment below on what you'd like to  see me discuss next subscribe to the  channel and i'll see you next time on  abby's kitchen bye  [Music]

